1
00:00:01,367 --> 00:00:11,830
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you have any direct method to overcome this sense of ownership?

2
00:00:12,290 --> 00:00:16,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Contemplate, through your ownership, that which you own.

3
00:00:17,911 --> 00:00:27,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So, take up that which you regard as yours, and then see how, or rather where, it is rooted at.

4
00:00:28,151 --> 00:00:31,572
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether in your sense of control, or whether in something else that it isn't yours.

5
00:00:33,073 --> 00:00:40,995
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for example, the body, take up the body, my body, is that sense of my body, is that the center of it?

6
00:00:41,675 --> 00:00:53,139
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, would this body be depending upon all these individual organs within that sense of ownership of this body, that are required to be operating, that I cannot control?

7
00:00:57,297 --> 00:01:04,683
[SPEAKER_01]: If those organs were to fail, would I be able to entertain the notion of this body as mine, if this body dies right... right in front of me?

8
00:01:04,703 --> 00:01:07,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's... You realize that you couldn't.

9
00:01:07,685 --> 00:01:16,612
[SPEAKER_01]: So you're perverting the order by regarding... by giving the priority of that sense of self, of my master, of this body.

10
00:01:16,752 --> 00:01:21,235
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you think about the individual organs, you don't think about them within that sense of master.

11
00:01:21,255 --> 00:01:23,217
[SPEAKER_01]: You think about left, right, before or after.

12
00:01:23,577 --> 00:01:24,758
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you don't uproot it.

13
00:01:26,117 --> 00:01:31,383
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the closest to a method would be to contemplate within this ownership.

14
00:01:32,504 --> 00:01:38,390
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you can't leave the ownership out and contemplate things rightly, because the whole point of contemplation is to experience the non-ownership.

15
00:01:40,795 --> 00:01:57,286
[SPEAKER_01]: And you experience the non-ownership when you see that this body, no matter how much it's in your control, its heart is rooted in that which you cannot even conceive controlling, such as heart, breathing lungs, pumping blood, operating organs, functioning brain.

16
00:01:57,566 --> 00:02:02,510
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't even conceive how it would be to control those things or have say in their operating.

17
00:02:02,570 --> 00:02:10,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, they are the fundamental basis for the notion of your body as a whole that you do own.

18
00:02:10,992 --> 00:02:11,552
[SPEAKER_00]: Mistaken.

19
00:02:11,652 --> 00:02:15,094
[SPEAKER_00]: Because we think we come, we, myself, comes first.

20
00:02:16,274 --> 00:02:17,215
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yeah.

21
00:02:17,375 --> 00:02:20,136
[SPEAKER_01]: I come first, thus this is for me, this is mine.

22
00:02:20,156 --> 00:02:20,916
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the other way around.

23
00:02:20,936 --> 00:02:24,518
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because I take all this to be mine, I develop a sense of I am.

24
00:02:26,839 --> 00:02:34,302
[SPEAKER_01]: But that sense of I am can be undone, not by denying it, or by saying, oh, there's no self, there's not really me, look, everything is changing, this is not... No.

25
00:02:34,522 --> 00:02:36,123
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to take up that sense of I am,

26
00:02:37,015 --> 00:02:40,858
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, see that it's founded upon things that cannot be yours.

27
00:02:41,459 --> 00:02:44,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, automatically, your experience is the experience of not-I-am.

28
00:02:45,402 --> 00:02:46,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Reversing the mistakes.

29
00:02:47,283 --> 00:02:47,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

30
00:02:47,563 --> 00:02:48,644
[SPEAKER_01]: Draining, basically.

31
00:02:48,684 --> 00:02:49,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Draining them.

32
00:02:49,325 --> 00:02:54,489
[SPEAKER_01]: The kind of the structure stays the same, but what has drained away is that appropriation from it.

33
00:02:56,270 --> 00:02:58,872
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha would say, for an arhant, it's like a palm stump.

34
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:00,571
[SPEAKER_01]: Cut at its root.

35
00:03:01,031 --> 00:03:04,354
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the stump, the branches that grown, they stayed, but now they're then done.

36
00:03:04,434 --> 00:03:05,135
[SPEAKER_01]: They're petrified.

37
00:03:05,435 --> 00:03:11,040
[SPEAKER_01]: They're still there, as the way person's experience might have proliferated throughout the ignorant life.

38
00:03:12,041 --> 00:03:21,730
[SPEAKER_01]: But, once you uproot that ignorance, once you remove that appropriation, it stops proliferating and then just stays like a palm stump, until even that breaks apart in parinibbāna.

39
00:03:22,895 --> 00:03:39,346
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if you lose that or get rid of that sense of ownership, your personality doesn't... how does your personality... Well, anything in your personality that was depending upon the ownership, that will disappear.

40
00:03:39,847 --> 00:03:42,028
[SPEAKER_01]: But there are other things that were not depending upon ownership.

41
00:03:42,068 --> 00:03:43,749
[SPEAKER_01]: They were depending upon like hollow habits.

42
00:03:44,470 --> 00:03:46,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Habits that were not rooted in greed, aversion, delusion.

43
00:03:46,811 --> 00:03:47,312
[SPEAKER_01]: That stays.

44
00:03:48,302 --> 00:03:53,604
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, as I said many times, and in the suttas you read often, people cannot tell an arahant externally.

45
00:03:55,305 --> 00:03:59,866
[SPEAKER_01]: And even another arahant cannot come and see the, I don't know, some special aura around him, oh, he's an arahant.

46
00:03:59,886 --> 00:04:00,006
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

47
00:04:00,327 --> 00:04:01,567
[SPEAKER_01]: They can tell an arahant when they start

48
00:04:01,873 --> 00:04:06,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Discussing each other's understanding of dhamma, and then you can see, oh yeah, he understood this, probably to the extent.

49
00:04:06,936 --> 00:04:16,521
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, then the Buddha, or somebody with very, very developed mind, could see little traits, little things, based on which he could tell the other person's mind.

50
00:04:17,161 --> 00:04:18,342
[SPEAKER_01]: But on an average, you can't.

51
00:04:18,362 --> 00:04:22,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Even like Sariputta couldn't tell, in some cases, that the person was an arahant.

52
00:04:22,884 --> 00:04:24,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you go to the Buddha and said,

53
00:04:24,404 --> 00:04:25,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Where was his rebirth?

54
00:04:25,325 --> 00:04:26,005
[SPEAKER_01]: And he says, what do you mean?

55
00:04:26,185 --> 00:04:27,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Didn't he say he was an arahant?

56
00:04:27,346 --> 00:04:32,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he did, but he was associating with some... not some reputable families and so on.

57
00:04:32,049 --> 00:04:34,150
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, well, that's... that's not why a person is blameworthy.

58
00:04:34,170 --> 00:04:41,835
[SPEAKER_01]: A person is blameworthy because they leave this body, they take another body, as in they keep... remain bound with saṃsāra.

59
00:04:41,895 --> 00:04:42,996
[SPEAKER_01]: But I can tell you he wasn't.

60
00:04:43,637 --> 00:04:44,457
[SPEAKER_01]: He wasn't blameworthy.

61
00:04:44,477 --> 00:04:46,138
[SPEAKER_01]: He was an arahant even before he died.

62
00:04:46,959 --> 00:04:47,879
[SPEAKER_01]: But Saripuri couldn't see it.

63
00:04:49,865 --> 00:04:55,710
[SPEAKER_01]: So the... or the other, that famous one, who was like really harsh in his speech, and the people were often offended.

64
00:04:56,430 --> 00:04:58,272
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Buddha said, oh, it's his old habits.

65
00:04:59,273 --> 00:05:02,015
[SPEAKER_01]: They were not rooted in ill will or greed or anything like that.

66
00:05:02,075 --> 00:05:06,018
[SPEAKER_01]: But obviously, he would come across as very angry, furious.

67
00:05:06,078 --> 00:05:14,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, that doesn't mean like, oh, okay, so I don't have to... You will know that... You will know which habits can stay once you're an arahant or on the way of becoming one.

68
00:05:15,306 --> 00:05:16,787
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't decide that beforehand.

69
00:05:16,867 --> 00:05:17,488
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, these are my...

70
00:05:18,202 --> 00:05:19,383
[SPEAKER_01]: Neutral habits.

71
00:05:19,603 --> 00:05:21,844
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to take them all with caution.

72
00:05:21,884 --> 00:05:25,186
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're in a state of putujana, you can't trust any of your habits.

73
00:05:25,206 --> 00:05:28,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to keep investigating any of it, if it's rooted on greed, aversion, delusion.

74
00:05:29,588 --> 00:05:35,111
[SPEAKER_01]: And truly, truly, when you dismantle everything that shouldn't be there, whatever's left, then you know that can be left.

75
00:05:36,451 --> 00:05:41,634
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's very different from saying that the self doesn't exist.

76
00:05:42,054 --> 00:05:44,055
[SPEAKER_00]: There is no self.

77
00:05:44,155 --> 00:05:45,216
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the Buddha never said that.

78
00:05:45,725 --> 00:05:47,226
[SPEAKER_01]: But he asked him, does the self exist?

79
00:05:47,246 --> 00:05:47,846
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't say it.

80
00:05:47,866 --> 00:05:48,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Does the self does not exist?

81
00:05:49,046 --> 00:05:49,827
[SPEAKER_01]: He wouldn't answer it.

82
00:05:50,927 --> 00:05:52,068
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's a... it's a shortcut.

83
00:05:52,288 --> 00:05:56,469
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a quick fix, in terms of satisfying explanation.

84
00:05:56,569 --> 00:05:58,490
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not a contemplation when you say there's no self.

85
00:05:59,170 --> 00:06:02,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Look, all these rocks, they're constantly changing electrons and atoms.

86
00:06:02,172 --> 00:06:02,992
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, nothing is real.

87
00:06:03,012 --> 00:06:03,832
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, there is no self.

88
00:06:03,852 --> 00:06:06,313
[SPEAKER_01]: This is just a hollow convention.

89
00:06:06,353 --> 00:06:07,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Conventional speech and so on.

90
00:06:08,454 --> 00:06:09,795
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's... that's not what Buddha said.

91
00:06:10,655 --> 00:06:11,435
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you can say that yourself.

92
00:06:11,455 --> 00:06:12,896
[SPEAKER_01]: Because conventions are real as such.

93
00:06:13,818 --> 00:06:18,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you might not call it self, but the thing is that phenomena that you refer to yourself, it's there.

94
00:06:18,461 --> 00:06:21,523
[SPEAKER_01]: If it weren't there, you wouldn't be able to refer to it whatsoever.

95
00:06:23,425 --> 00:06:27,187
[SPEAKER_01]: So, dismissing the reality does not remove your ownership of that reality.

96
00:06:30,049 --> 00:06:38,235
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, ah, so I should be concerned with me appropriating reality, not being concerned about dismantling conventions and proving that nothing exists.

97
00:06:41,413 --> 00:06:46,357
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, instead of treating the illness in regard to the body, you just go and chop up the body.

98
00:06:47,518 --> 00:06:50,741
[SPEAKER_01]: And say, see, the body can be chopped up, thus it's not real.

99
00:06:51,341 --> 00:06:52,742
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it is real.

100
00:06:52,782 --> 00:06:54,784
[SPEAKER_01]: As a collection of parts that can be chopped up.

101
00:06:55,384 --> 00:06:56,845
[SPEAKER_01]: That's a reality.

102
00:06:56,886 --> 00:07:00,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And that was never a problem.

103
00:07:00,608 --> 00:07:02,010
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is that self.

104
00:07:02,210 --> 00:07:03,371
[SPEAKER_01]: There is that sense of self.

105
00:07:04,812 --> 00:07:05,953
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not that there is no self.

106
00:07:06,853 --> 00:07:18,179
[SPEAKER_01]: But, what you should do is remove the ownership of that sense of self, and then it becomes anatta, as in not myself.

107
00:07:20,980 --> 00:07:22,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it's a practice of denial.

108
00:07:23,641 --> 00:07:27,323
[SPEAKER_01]: And actually, I don't want to say not myself, because then people might assume, ah, someone else's self.

109
00:07:28,043 --> 00:07:28,163
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

110
00:07:28,404 --> 00:07:33,206
[SPEAKER_01]: Anatta is self that you don't own, and that cannot be called self.

111
00:07:34,106 --> 00:07:35,267
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not self.

112
00:07:36,452 --> 00:07:40,153
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that which was taken as self, still there, but now it's not.

113
00:07:40,413 --> 00:07:41,513
[SPEAKER_01]: Taken as not-self.

114
00:07:43,133 --> 00:07:49,575
[SPEAKER_01]: But people are concerned with denying that which is taken as self, which is an aspect of their experience, one way or the other.

115
00:07:50,355 --> 00:07:56,876
[SPEAKER_01]: But as the Buddha said, the five aggregates are not the assumption, are not the holding the view of the self.

116
00:07:57,676 --> 00:08:00,437
[SPEAKER_01]: But there is no view of the self apart from the five aggregates.

117
00:08:01,377 --> 00:08:04,899
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's desire and lust in regard to the five aggregates that the view of self is there.

118
00:08:05,399 --> 00:08:13,783
[SPEAKER_01]: By you coming and saying there is no self, the five aggregates are impermanent, there is no self, you're just dismissing it, the problem, and without actually understanding it.

119
00:08:13,803 --> 00:08:21,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were to... if you were to truly understand it, well, the only way to truly understand it is to free yourself from that self, to see it as not-self.

120
00:08:21,647 --> 00:08:26,469
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any explanation that you think you understood, unless it's freeing you from that sense of self, don't think you understood it.

121
00:08:28,943 --> 00:08:33,925
[SPEAKER_00]: Because people can put a lot of time and effort in denial practice.

122
00:08:33,965 --> 00:08:35,566
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, unfortunately.

123
00:08:35,706 --> 00:08:39,287
[SPEAKER_01]: Many of the meditation methods and techniques are all based on that view.

124
00:08:39,307 --> 00:08:46,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Just denial and seeing the moment-to-moment movement, they will kind of show you that somehow things are not really real, cannot be yours.

125
00:08:46,590 --> 00:08:50,512
[SPEAKER_00]: But that whole practice goes completely against being

126
00:08:52,329 --> 00:08:58,458
[SPEAKER_00]: Self-transparent, honest, authentic, saying this, I do experience this self, I do experience this thing.

127
00:08:58,478 --> 00:09:01,662
[SPEAKER_01]: There is mother, there is father, there is given, there is offered.

128
00:09:01,782 --> 00:09:02,563
[SPEAKER_00]: There's no gender.

129
00:09:02,944 --> 00:09:05,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, well, okay, people can deny anything, whatever they please.

130
00:09:06,912 --> 00:09:08,873
[SPEAKER_01]: As the attitude, yeah, it can be aimed literally at anything.

131
00:09:08,893 --> 00:09:16,975
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said the right... the worldly right view is the view that acknowledges the validity of random phenomena, of phenomena in your experience.

132
00:09:17,015 --> 00:09:35,201
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, there is this, there is that, there is given, there is the... there is mother, there is father, there is the liberation, there is the path, allowing... Like, you don't need to see them, and of course, you don't see them at that level, but you need to know that there is a possibility of them being... being real, as in there is a possibility of being free.

133
00:09:36,754 --> 00:09:40,977
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you keep denying everything, you will never accept that possibility as a present phenomenon.

134
00:09:41,258 --> 00:09:41,918
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the key.

135
00:09:42,499 --> 00:09:46,222
[SPEAKER_01]: Accepting possibilities as a present negative phenomenon in your experience.

136
00:09:47,082 --> 00:09:47,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, it's real.

137
00:09:47,903 --> 00:09:48,944
[SPEAKER_01]: The possibility is real.

138
00:09:49,324 --> 00:09:53,467
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know how to what extent, but I could find out if I apply myself.

139
00:09:56,170 --> 00:09:59,152
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't want to deny anything, really.

140
00:09:59,212 --> 00:10:01,994
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to restrain yourself from what you were told it's unwholesome.

141
00:10:02,975 --> 00:10:05,177
[SPEAKER_01]: Greed averse delusion that you can see that it's unwholesome.

142
00:10:06,190 --> 00:10:09,171
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, within that, you don't need to deny further.

143
00:10:09,191 --> 00:10:10,712
[SPEAKER_01]: That would be like overshooting the mark.

144
00:10:11,372 --> 00:10:13,132
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to understand what's in front of you.

145
00:10:13,152 --> 00:10:13,813
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what Buddha said.

146
00:10:14,173 --> 00:10:20,055
[SPEAKER_01]: The whole universe is contained within this less than six foot long body and so on.

147
00:10:21,098 --> 00:10:26,604
[SPEAKER_01]: He didn't say his body is not real, thus none of the universe is real, thus there is no travel, there is no mother, there is no father, there is no life.

148
00:10:27,084 --> 00:10:30,348
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there were those views at the time that he quite heavily criticized.

149
00:10:31,269 --> 00:10:39,578
[SPEAKER_01]: Ah, nobody gets killed, there's nothing, it's just elements, just... And actually, modern interpretation of Buddhist anatta, it's actually one of those wrong views back in the day that the Buddha criticized.

150
00:10:40,078 --> 00:10:46,400
[SPEAKER_01]: When they say everything is comprised of constantly changing electrons, so nothing is real or nothing can be mine, there is no self.

151
00:10:47,180 --> 00:10:50,261
[SPEAKER_01]: And people believe that, whereby they said, oh, nobody gets killed.

152
00:10:50,341 --> 00:10:55,422
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you go and cut everybody on the left side of Ganges with a sharp sword, nobody gets killed.

153
00:10:55,462 --> 00:11:01,604
[SPEAKER_01]: All that happens is the sword runs through the elements, runs through the electrons and atoms.

154
00:11:01,664 --> 00:11:03,084
[SPEAKER_01]: You can just call it whatever you want.

155
00:11:03,344 --> 00:11:04,305
[SPEAKER_01]: The view is the same.

156
00:11:05,385 --> 00:11:06,685
[SPEAKER_01]: And they will say it's completely wrong view.

157
00:11:07,545 --> 00:11:09,146
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're blaming the five aggregates.

158
00:11:09,961 --> 00:11:15,468
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, person who is not freed from the sense of self, when the five aggregates disintegrate, as in when they die, they're still there.

159
00:11:29,988 --> 00:11:31,529
[SPEAKER_01]: They get reborn.

160
00:11:31,769 --> 00:11:37,574
[SPEAKER_00]: It reminds me of when someone's got a problem and it's like, you know, complaining about trying to talk it out.

161
00:11:38,434 --> 00:11:40,156
[SPEAKER_00]: And then, oh, I don't know the answer.

162
00:11:40,236 --> 00:11:42,718
[SPEAKER_00]: But anyway, anyway, everything's impermanent.

163
00:11:42,758 --> 00:11:44,839
[SPEAKER_00]: So, anyway, it's OK.

164
00:11:44,859 --> 00:11:45,500
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like this.

165
00:11:46,340 --> 00:11:46,681
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, well.

166
00:11:47,521 --> 00:11:50,704
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, I've got this problem, but I'll just deny it now.

167
00:11:50,864 --> 00:11:58,830
[SPEAKER_01]: No, unfortunately, it's prevalent as a view, but it's just rooted in lazy self-reflection.

168
00:12:00,057 --> 00:12:02,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Like in self-reflection, it doesn't go far enough.

169
00:12:05,521 --> 00:12:11,226
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you just put a cap on it by saying, ah, well, it's all impermanent anyway, so I won't bother with it.

170
00:12:13,308 --> 00:12:17,010
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just, you know, watch my breath and be peaceful and live a simple life.

171
00:12:17,691 --> 00:12:25,677
[SPEAKER_01]: You can live life as simply as you want, as a vegetable, and still remain equally ignorant.

172
00:12:25,697 --> 00:12:26,538
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the problem.

173
00:12:26,958 --> 00:12:28,800
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not how complex your life is.

174
00:12:29,361 --> 00:12:37,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Of course, you want to simplify it, because it can be too much, like, to start with, to practice, but that's secondary.

175
00:12:37,644 --> 00:12:41,186
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just creating the suitable condition for you to understand what's right in front of you.

176
00:12:42,346 --> 00:12:58,092
[SPEAKER_01]: But thinking that the goal of your life is to simplify, or to just do this, just do that, without making the effort to understand it, and go through that unpleasantness of unraveling the deep existential feelings of fear and anxiety, yeah, there's no practice without that.

177
00:13:03,993 --> 00:13:04,840
It's not like that.

178
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:05,808
It's not like that.

